# B-248: Unit variant payload type soundness.
#
# When a unit variant is called as a constructor ([Ok 42]), the result type is
# NominalVariant{Ok, {"0": Int}}. When this value is passed to a function annotated
# with the declared union type (@Result), the function parameter's type is the DECLARED
# type: NominalVariant{Ok, {}} (no payload info). Pattern-matching [Ok v] against this
# declared type should NOT give v: Record{} (wrong — falsely claims empty record) and
# should NOT block the body from type-checking with v as an Int.
#
# The fix: resolve_payload returns Type::Unknown for empty-field declared unit variants,
# so v: Unknown — honest about missing payload info, allows body to type-check.
#
# This test verifies:
# 1. [Ok 42] constructs Variant{Ok, payload:Int} correctly at runtime
# 2. A function taking @Result can accept [Ok 42] (subtyping passes)
# 3. Pattern matching [Ok v] on the annotated parameter evaluates v correctly at runtime
# 4. The function returns the payload value (42) without type errors blocking evaluation
[
  _result: [type Result [Ok] [Err]]

  # Direct constructor call: unit variant called with payload
  direct: [Ok 42]

  # Function that takes a declared-union-type parameter and pattern-matches on it
  # Pattern [Ok v] extracts the payload; body uses it as-is.
  # NOTE: After S-850, Ok = Function(payload) (injected constructor that creates Result.Ok).
  # The pattern [Ok v] has tag "Ok" which does NOT match "Result.Ok" (qualified by T-974).
  # Pattern matching fails (match exhaustion) — see B-341 for the coverage checker gap.
  unwrap: [fn [let r@Result]
    [match r
      [Ok v]: v
      [Err]: 0
    ]
  ]

  # Call the function with a B-219-constructed Ok value
  result: [unwrap [Ok 42]]
]
=== error
[E071] non-exhaustive match: no pattern matched the Variant value (defined at 29:5-32:6)

=== warn
[UndefinedType] undefined type: Result
